<h1>Susana Mendoza</h1>
<p>Illinois Comptroller. Candidate for mayor of Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027.</p>
<p>State Comptroller since 2016, Chicago City Clerk before that, and a state representative before that; 54, grew up in Little Village and lives in Portage Park with her husband and son. Filed 26,000 signatures on the last day, in a tamale cart.</p>
<p><a href="/directdemocracy/app/candidate/xJE4JOm5uIgUG16LnC67XvZEUZl1">Full platform, open to comments</a></p>
<p><a href="https://susanamendoza.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website (susanamendoza.com)</a></p>
<h2>What sets them apart</h2>
<p>Only Mendoza: Change the state&#39;s SAFE-T Act so people accused of murder or predatory sexual assault cannot be released on electronic monitoring.</p>
<p>TIF money: Stop using neighborhood TIF funds to balance the budget. Quigley agrees; Johnson sends the surplus to public schools.</p>
<p>Pensions: Pay pensions ahead whenever revenue beats forecasts, as her state office did. Quigley and Cardenas would have workers share the cost.</p>
<p>Also: Extend the Riverwalk to Chinatown and Little Village, and post Chicago&#39;s permit times next to other cities&#39;.</p>
<h2>Where they split from the field</h2>
<p>Pensions: Restructure the funds and pay ahead, with Giannoulias. Workers share the cost: Quigley and Cardenas. Change the state constitution: Wilson. Bankruptcy on the table: Kitover.</p>
<p>TIF money: Keep it in the neighborhoods it came from, with Quigley. Send the surplus to public schools: Johnson.</p>
<h2>In their words</h2>
<ul>
<li>Change the state&#39;s SAFE-T Act so people accused of murder or predatory sexual assault cannot be released on electronic monitoring. <q>The SAFE-T Act needs to be amended by repealing this provision.</q> (<a href="https://susanamendoza.com/priorities/public-safety/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign platform (susanamendoza.com)</a>)</li>
<li>Stop using neighborhood TIF funds to balance the city budget. <q>They were not created to be slush funds that a mayor or council snatches to balance a budget.</q> (<a href="https://susanamendoza.com/priorities/finances/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign platform (susanamendoza.com)</a>)</li>
<li>Cut housing approval and permitting times in half and get cranes back up across the city. <q>As mayor, I will make the construction crane the official bird of Chicago,</q> (<a href="https://chicago.suntimes.com/elections/2026/10/28/mayor-election-mendoza-city-club-brandon-johnson-proposed-budget-head-tax-business-community" rel="nofollow noopener">City Club speech, Oct. 28, 2026 (Sun-Times)</a>)</li>
<li>Extend the Riverwalk to Chinatown and Little Village. <q>I&#39;m gonna like lean into one of my dreams, and that is to grow beyond the current Riverwalk,</q> (<a href="https://chi.streetsblog.org/2026/11/04/mayoral-hopeful-mendoza-discusses-one-of-my-dreams-extending-the-riverwalk-to-chinatown-and-little-village" rel="nofollow noopener">Streetsblog Chicago, Nov. 4, 2026</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Campaign money</h2>
<ul>
<li>Committee: Susana For Chicago</li>
<li>Raised: $277K</li>
<li>Spent: $416K</li>
<li>Cash on hand: $1.7M</li>
<li>Raised July 1 to Sept. 30, 2026; spent Jan. 1 to Sept. 30, 2026. <a href="https://elections.il.gov/CampaignDisclosure/CommitteeDetail.aspx?id=18308" rel="nofollow noopener">Source</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Money figures are each committee&#39;s quarterly reports to the Illinois State Board of Elections: spent is total expenditures from January 1 to September 30, 2026 (or since the committee formed), cash is funds available on September 30.</p>
<h2>Support</h2>
<p>9.5% of likely voters in the ChicagoSpeaks poll, Sept. 2026 (Mansueto Institute / NORC). 23 points of support per $1 million spent.</p>
<h2>On money</h2>
<p>Would grow the economy rather than tax it, review every line of the budget, meet the credit rating agencies on her first morning, and treat businesses as partners instead of piggy banks.</p>
<ul>
<li><q>I can&#39;t wait to get my hands into that budget, look over every line item,</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/illinois-comptroller-susana-mendoza-mayoral-chicago/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, June 3, 2026</a>)</li>
<li><q>Chicago businesses deserve to stop feeling like piggy banks and start feeling like partners.</q> (<a href="https://chicago.suntimes.com/elections/2026/10/28/mayor-election-mendoza-city-club-brandon-johnson-proposed-budget-head-tax-business-community" rel="nofollow noopener">City Club speech, Oct. 28, 2026 (Sun-Times)</a>)</li>
<li><q>In the morning, I&#39;m gonna meet with every single credit rating agency</q> (<a href="https://chicago.suntimes.com/elections/2026/10/28/mayor-election-mendoza-city-club-brandon-johnson-proposed-budget-head-tax-business-community" rel="nofollow noopener">City Club speech, Oct. 28, 2026 (Sun-Times)</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pizza</h2>
<p>No position on record.</p>
<h2>Also on the record</h2>
<ul>
<li>An all-state soccer midfielder in high school who played at Truman State, with 68 appearances and 10 goals. (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susana_Mendoza" rel="nofollow noopener">en.wikipedia.org</a>)</li>
<li>Delivered her nominating petitions in a tamale cart. (<a href="https://chicago.suntimes.com/elections/2026/10/26/chicago-mayoral-race-last-day-of-filing-mendoza-pappas-2027-election" rel="nofollow noopener">chicago.suntimes.com</a>)</li>
</ul>
